            <ArticleTitle>Measuring patient reported outcomes of acupuncture treatment on pain patients' health status.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>192-199</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1744-3881(16)30218-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ctcp.2017.06.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The primary aim of this observational multicenter case series study was to assess patient reported outcome measures of health status following routine acupuncture treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Via the patient reported 'MYMOP2-online' questionnaire 110 pain patients reported three times (baseline, after four weeks, after sixteen weeks) whether changes in health status occurred following un-standardized routine acupuncture treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">ANOVA for repeated measures showed that primary pain (-38.8%), secondary pain (-38.4%), limitation to daily functioning (-33.2%), subjective well-being (24.2%), and health status (34.0%) significantly improved between zero and four weeks (p &lt; 0.001). The achieved health benefits persisted over the long term (sixteen weeks). Pain medication usage decreased by 44.5% in four weeks. The changes in health status could not be related to specific aspects of acupuncture treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In this observational study pain patients self-reported a statistically significant and clinically relevant long-term improvement in health status following acupuncture treatments in routine practice.</AbstractText>
